# Migrating `BackgroundProcessor` to .NET 8

## Key Breaking Change

**`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 5+.** `Thread.ResetAbort()` is also removed. This is the #1 pitfall — your `Stop()` method will crash at runtime.

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private Task? _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = await GetNextItemAsync(token); // pass token to unblock waits
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Graceful shutdown requested
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerTask is null)
            return;

        _cts.Cancel();

        // Wait with timeout instead of Thread.Sleep + Abort
        var effectiveTimeout = timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
        if (await Task.WhenAny(_workerTask, Task.Delay(effectiveTimeout)) != _workerTask)
        {
            // Log warning: worker did not exit in time
            // No forced kill — design GetNextItemAsync to respect the token
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await StopAsync();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| **`Thread.Abort` removed** | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`. Replace with `CancellationToken` cooperative cancellation. |
| **Blocking `GetNextItem()`** | If this blocks on a `BlockingCollection<T>`, use `TakeAsync` or pass the token to `collection.Take(token)` so cancellation actually unblocks. |
| **`volatile bool` race** | A flag alone can't interrupt a blocked call. `CancellationToken` wires into async I/O, semaphores, and channels natively. |
| **`Thread.Sleep` in Stop** | Replaced with `Task.WhenAny` + timeout — non-blocking and deterministic. |
| **Fire-and-forget loss** | Using `Task.Run` without storing the task swallows exceptions. Always store and await. |
| **`ThreadAbortException` catch** | This type still exists but is never thrown. Remove the catch or replace with `OperationCanceledException`. |

## Migration Checklist

1. Replace `Thread` + `volatile bool` → `Task` + `CancellationTokenSource`
2. Replace `Thread.Abort()` → `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()`
3. Replace `ThreadAbortException` catch → `OperationCanceledException` catch
4. Thread blocking calls (`GetNextItem`) → async equivalents accepting `CancellationToken`
5. Implement `IAsyncDisposable` for deterministic cleanup
6. If using `BlockingCollection<T>`, consider migrating to `System.Threading.Channels`

## Bonus: Channel-based alternative for `GetNextItem`

```csharp
private readonly Channel<WorkItem> _channel = Channel.CreateBounded<WorkItem>(100);

private async Task ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
{
    await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(token))
    {
        await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
    }
}
```

This is the idiomatic .NET 8 pattern — back-pressure, cancellation, and async enumeration built in.
